jpeg: flush buffered frames when resuming an idle encoder

The JPEG worker pauses its encoder after a snapshot so an idle preview does
not burn cycles. IMP_Encoder_StopRecvPic stops the acquisition but, as the
IMP documentation states, the stream buffer is not eliminated. The frames
left in that buffer stay there until they are read, and when the worker is
woken by the next snapshot it reads those frames first. The IPC handler
waits for frame_seq to advance, so it accepts the stale frame as fresh and
a motion alert can carry a snapshot from the previous event, hours earlier.

Flush the encoder right after restarting it, the same way VideoWorker clears
its stream, so the next captured frame is encoded after the wake-up.

diff --git a/src/video/JPEGWorker.cpp b/src/video/JPEGWorker.cpp
index 600b5d1..7ab6255 100644
--- a/src/video/JPEGWorker.cpp
+++ b/src/video/JPEGWorker.cpp
@@ -351,6 +351,15 @@ void JPEGWorker::run() {
       if (global_jpeg[jpgChn]->encoder_paused) {
         IMP_Encoder_StartRecvPic(global_jpeg[jpgChn]->encChn);
         global_jpeg[jpgChn]->encoder_paused = false;
+        // StopRecvPic leaves the encoded frames in the channel buffer. Without
+        // a flush the resumed worker reads those first and serves a snapshot
+        // from the previous request, which can be hours old.
+        int flush_ret = IMP_Encoder_FlushStream(global_jpeg[jpgChn]->encChn);
+        if (flush_ret != 0) {
+          LOG_WARN("jpeg" << jpgChn << ": IMP_Encoder_FlushStream("
+                          << global_jpeg[jpgChn]->encChn
+                          << ") failed: " << flush_ret);
+        }
         LOG_INFO("jpeg" << jpgChn << ": encoder resumed");
       }
 
